\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For an irreducible smooth quasi-projective variety $S$ with a variation $V$ of mixed rational Hodge structures, is the atypical locus $S_{\mathrm{atyp}}(V)$ a finite union of maximal atypical subvarieties, equivalently a proper algebraic subset and hence not Zariski dense; and, in the generalized André–Oort case, does Zariski density of CM points force $V$ to arise by pullback from a connected mixed Shimura variety?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2016/38}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For an irreducible smooth quasi-projective variety $S$ with a variation $V$ of mixed rational Hodge structures, is the atypical locus $S_{\mathrm{atyp}}(V)$ a finite union of maximal atypical subvarieties, equivalently a proper algebraic subset and hence not Zariski dense; and, in the generalized André–Oort case, does Zariski density of CM points force $V$ to arise by pullback from a connected mixed Shimura variety?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30003198 (OWR-14746-013). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
